Implementation status and remaining gaps (milestones B-H complete):

  - bitfields, CAS, CAS2, CHK2/CMP2 and BTST Dn,#imm are all implemented
    (CAS/CAS2 are not bus locked: single CPU master on this fabric)
  - trace: T1 (all instructions) and T0 (change of flow plus the 68040
    pipeline-synchronizing list such as NOP, SR/control-register changes,
    cache/MMU maintenance, FMOVEM/FRESTORE and STOP) generate format $2
    vector 9 exceptions on completion. A traced STOP does not enter the
    stopped state. An instruction that itself raises an exception (TRAP,
    etc.) leaves its trace disposition to that exception's handler.
  - TAS is not bus-locked; table-search descriptor updates are plain
    read/write cycles (single-master fabric)
  - physical bus errors stack a format $7 frame with the SSW ATC bit clear,
    abort the active 16-bit adapter/chip-bus cycle, and are generated by a
    2^20-clk_sys watchdog (about 36.6 ms at 28.6875 MHz).  This is deliberately far
    longer than chip RAM's legitimate DMA-slot wait.  t_exceptions injects
    berr and verifies the frame plus restart; tb_ap040_bus_timeout verifies
    the watchdog boundary, persistence and reset
  - reset/exception processing fetches four complete longwords before
    handler execution.  The eight words are retained and consumed without
    duplicate bus reads; a fault on any word is a double bus fault.  Fatal
    halt cancels all CPU/MMU/cache-maintenance request sidebands and the
    external bus remains idle until reset.  RTE frame-load faults remain
    double faults as required by MC68040UM 8.2
  - denied accesses (supervisor-page violation or write protection) set the
    page descriptor's U bit before faulting, without setting M.  The MMU
    waits for descriptor writeback, raises c_flt, then waits for the core to
    drop the held request before returning to W_IDLE.  t_mmu's protected-page
    handler records the pre-fix descriptor and verifies U was set
  - transfers that cross a page boundary are split into byte transactions
    so each byte is translated through its own page (t_mmu tests 40-44);
    only misaligned transfers can cross
  - every multiword transfer also presents each 16-bit physical subcycle as
    a separate external request. cpu_cache_new and the SDRAM/DDR3 write
    buffers retain their accepted address while acknowledge is high, so the
    adapter inserts one sampled IDLE clock before presenting the next word.
    tb_ap040_bus16_gap proves both halves of reads and writes are accepted
    exactly once under that level-acknowledge contract
  - interrupts are always autovectored (the wrapper ties ipl_autovector=1)
  - format $7 frames carry SSW and the initial FA. EA normally mirrors FA
    (line-aligned for MOVE16); a MOVEM operand fault sets CM and saves its
    original EA. RTE consumes CM/EA for indexed and PC-relative restart.
    WB3 validity stays clear because the restart model repeats the store.
    t_mmu.s and t_movem_restart.s cover fault/restart and frame handling
  - caches are write-through, so CPUSH equals CINV and no copyback/push state
    exists. Aligned cacheable store hits update only their matching resident
    data-cache word after the external write acknowledges; complex and
    cache-inhibited stores invalidate their touched sets. CINV/CPUSH invalidate
    the whole selected cache regardless of scope (architecturally safe);
    misaligned and line-crossing reads bypass the caches; the I-cache is not
    snooped by CPU writes (real 68040 behavior: software must CINV)
  - the Minimig build sets AP040_ENABLE_CACHE=0 in cpu_wrapper.v: the
    internal caches are compiled out (CINV/CPUSH complete immediately,
    which is a valid 68040 whose caches never fill) and cpu_cache_new in
    the RAM controllers provides snooped caching instead. Simulation
    keeps the caches enabled (compat default is 1) so t_cache.s stays
    meaningful.  CACR enables reach the external I and D banks independently;
    CINV/CPUSH crosses as a retained toggle and clears both external caches
    (safe over-invalidation for selected/scope variants). tb_cpu_cache_new
    verifies I/D isolation, disabled-fill suppression and maintenance clear
  - misaligned accesses restart the whole transfer. A second-page ATC
    fault reports the original transfer address/size and sets SSW.MA;
    failed searches install nonresident ATC entries until invalidation or
    replacement. t_atcprobe.s covers invalid/protected/PTEST-failed entries,
    instruction/data isolation, 8K pages and PFLUSH/PTEST invalidation
  - FPU (milestone H): ap040_fpu.v implements FPCR/FPSR/FPIAR, B/W/L/S/
    D/X movement and conversion, FABS/FNEG/FTST/FCMP, and hardware
    FADD/FSUB/FMUL/FDIV/FSQRT. It supports FPCR rounding/precision,
    gradual single/double store underflow, canonical extended special
    values, SNAN/BSUN/DZ and enabled arithmetic-exception vectors.
    FMOVEM register/control paths, FBcc/FScc/FDBcc/FTRAPcc and NULL/IDLE/
    UNIMP FSAVE/FRESTORE frames are covered by t_fpu.s. Transcendentals and
    FMOVECR are unimplemented instructions (vector 11, format $2);
    denormal/unnormal operands and packed decimal are unsupported data
    types and take vector 55 in a format-$3 frame. Opclass 000/010 sources
    stack the faulting PC and their source EA (zero for register/immediate);
    opclass 011 stores stack the next PC and calculated destination EA.
    FSGLMUL chops its source mantissas to 24 bits; FSGLDIV does not.
    Underflowed results take the denormalized encoding (exponent field
    zero, significand shifted right by 1 - exponent), which is what
    softfloat and so the cputest reference data define; UNFL and INEX
    are signalled with them. Arithmetic rounding precision controls both
    significand precision and exponent range; extended precision and the
    FSGL class retain the extended exponent range.
    Unimplemented instructions retain the revision-$41 52-byte UNIMP
    state: CMDREG1B/CMDREG3B, STAG/DTAG, E1 and the 96-bit FPTEMP/ETEMP
    operands. FSAVE writes and acknowledges that payload only after its
    final successful bus transfer; FRESTORE reads the complete payload
    before installing it. Full-frame -(An)/(An)+ adjustment is covered,
    along with exact register-source and converted memory-source images.
    This supplies the operand state a stock Motorola FPSP expects for
    vector-11 software instructions such as transcendentals and FMOVECR.
    FRESTORE accepts NULL, IDLE and revision-
    $41 UNIMP frames and raises vector 14 for an incompatible version or
    size. Remaining state-frame deviations are the vector-55 datatype
    variant (the exception frame is correct, but FSAVE does not yet expose
    its source payload) and the 96-byte BUSY form; AP040 has no pipelined
    mid-instruction arithmetic state to serialize. Real 040 silicon also takes non-maskable OVFL/UNFL traps so
    the FPSP can produce these results, where AP040 computes them
    directly. The FPGA build enables AP040_HAS_FPU=1.
  - 8K page mode (TC.P) is covered by t_mmu.s including LA bit 12
    routing, PTEST and fault restart

Gaps closed 2026-08-05
----------------------
The bus-error, denied-access U-bit, independent external I/D enable and
external CINV/CPUSH gaps recorded here on 2026-08-04/05 are implemented and
covered by negative-control regressions.  CPU function codes are still left
unconnected at cpu_wrapper because the current Minimig fabric has no FC
consumer; they remain available at ap040_tg68k_compat.fc if one is added.
HRTMon receives AP040's retained level-7 acceptance toggle, so the cartridge
