FPU rounding corrections 2026-08-05
-----------------------------------
Two fixes in ap040_fpu.v, both driven by hardware cputest 68040_basicfpu
and confirmed against M68000PRM.pdf:

  - FABS and FNEG round their result to the selected precision and report
    INEX2 plus accrued INEX whenever nonzero bits are discarded.  This is
    also true for an already-extended register source.  The earlier
    source-format exemption was disproved by 68040_basicfpu FABS.X/0001
    with FPCR $40, which requires FPSR $00000208.  WinUAE's cputest oracle
    confirms why: floatx80_abs/neg both call roundAndPackFloatx80, whose
    single- and double-precision paths raise inexact on nonzero round bits.
    The PRM operation-table shorthand saying INEX2 is "Cleared" does not
    describe the reference behavior.  t_fpu 113/116/130 cover double and
    reserved-as-double precision; 134 covers a converted double source;
    245-252 reproduce the exact register-source single-precision FABS and
    FNEG cases captured from hardware.

  - Range control restored for ARITHMETIC: the rounding precision narrows
    the exponent range as well as the significand.  Working bounds (bias
    16383) are single 16257..16510, double 15361..17406, extended
    1..32766.  Overflow saturates at that precision's largest normal;
    underflow keeps that precision's minimum exponent with a denormalized
    significand, and only extended underflow uses the exponent-field-zero
    denormal encoding.  t_fpu 120-125, overflow and underflow separately
    negative-control proven.
    The move-class exemption originally implemented here was REVERTED on
    2026-08-06: softfloat's floatx80_move/abs/neg go through
    roundAndPackFloatx80 and range-control like everything else, and the
    cputest reference is generated from softfloat (on silicon the case
    traps nonmaskably into the FPSP, which computes the range-controlled
    result too, so the PRM's "OVFL Cleared" prose describes neither).
    t_fpu 126-129 now assert the softfloat behaviour.  FSGLMUL/FSGLDIV
    are the true exemption: they round through roundSigAndPackFloatx80,
    which keeps the EXTENDED exponent range (op_sgl; t_fpu 153-155).
    FABS/FNEG use the same INEX2-producing rounder as FMOVE; there is no
    extended-source suppression.
    This reverts a change made on 2026-08-04 that judged both against the
    extended range.  That change came from reading the WRONG definition of
    roundAndPackFloatx80: softfloat.cpp has two, and the one AP040 must
    follow is the `#else // SOFTFLOAT_68K` copy (~line 1349) with its
    expOffset, not the `#ifndef SOFTFLOAT_68K` copy (~line 1108).  qemu
    behaves like the non-68k variant, which is why the FP differential
    appeared to justify the change; WinUAE plus the PRM outrank it.

F_UNFL also rounded extended-precision underflow at the double boundary
(dropping 11 bits); it now rounds at the guard/round/sticky boundary for
extended and at the precision boundary otherwise.

Turbo chip-RAM split-transfer corrections 2026-08-07
-----------------------------------------------------
Hardware cputest exposed two deterministic corruptions below the CPU core:
`fadd.p ([D0.w*8]),fp1` expected format-$3 EA $00000AA1 but stacked
$80090AA1, and `fabs.x ([0]),fp3` read its odd-addressed 12-byte operand
from a +2-byte window. The EA engine and FPU loader were correct.

Two independent request-boundary defects were fixed:

  - ap040_bus16_adapter changed addr_out directly between halves of a split
    transfer while keeping the external request asserted. Minimig's RAM
    targets use a level acknowledge and accept a new address only after
    cpu_cs drops, so the adapter now presents a sampled IDLE boundary.
    tb_ap040_bus16_gap negative-controls aligned and odd longword reads and
    writes (two word requests, or byte+word+byte respectively).
  - Minimig.sv's legacy early-CS logic dropped ram_cs for one clk_114 after
    ready, then reasserted it while cpu_wrapper still held ram_sel. That
    launched a ghost cpu_cache_new fill whose late data could acknowledge
    the following 16-bit subcycle. ram_cs_guard now latches the completed
    deselection until ram_sel drops. In the full turbo-SDRAM simulation,
    old behavior hangs four of the eight physically valid clk_28/cyc phase
    relationships; the guard passes all eight. The standard suite uses one
    of the old failing phases as its negative control.

t_fpu 253-255 reproduces the packed-operand frame, and 256-259 reproduces
the exact FABS.X indirect encoding and byte window captured on hardware.

WinUAE-oracle FPU audit 2026-08-06
----------------------------------
Requested comparison of the FPU against /home/adam/Musashi and
/home/adam/WinUAE to pass cputest.  Musashi turned out to be a generic
softfloat port (sign-set default NaN, no 040 quirks) and was set aside;
WinUAE generates the cputest reference data and is the oracle.  Dataset
insight that scopes the work: cputestgen.ini excludes vectors 48-54 from
the FPU sets and the generated FPCR never enables traps, so every case
that would raise the 040's NONMASKABLE OVFL/UNFL is filtered out at
generation time -- untrapped status-setting cases (DZ, OPERR, SNAN,
INEX2) remain in the data and are compared exactly.

Fixed in ap040_fpu.v (each negative-control proven, t_fpu 138 on):
  - FDIV special-case order: dividend-infinity precedes the divisor-zero
    check, so inf/0 is a clean infinity with NO DZ (t_fpu 138-140).
  - The default NaN is POSITIVE all-ones (0/0, inf/inf, inf-inf, 0*inf,
    sqrt of a negative); AP040 had kept the operand sign (141-146).
  - Infinity mantissas: created infinities are all-zero; pass-through
    infinities keep the operand's raw bits.  inf_clear_intbit is a 68060
    flag -- the 040 never canonicalizes to the 6888x 0x8000... form
    (62, 140, 147-148).
  - FCMP: equal nonzero finite values compare as +0 (N clear even for
    two negatives); equal zeros and infinities keep the DEST sign in N;
    a NaN compare reports the propagated NaN's sign in N (dest NaN
    preferred; the 040's cmp_signed_nan flag); a denormal DESTINATION
    faults v55, checked before any SNaN bookkeeping (149-152).
  - FSGLDIV does NOT chop its operands (only FSGLMUL does); both keep
    the extended exponent range via roundSigAndPackFloatx80 and saturate
    at the full all-ones mantissa (88-90, 153-155).
  - OVFL sets INEX2 only when mantissa bits were actually discarded;
    the accrued-INEX bit is set by OVFL regardless (126-127).
  - Accrued UNFL only accrues when the result is also inexact.
  - FPCR write mask on the 040 is $FFFF, not $FFF0 (156).
  - Integer stores saturate SIGN-dependently ($7FFF.../$8000...); a NaN
    stores its quieted payload top bits, raising OPERR only when it was
    already quiet and SNAN when signaling; S/D stores of a NaN force the
    quiet bit and record SNAN for signaling sources.
  - FMOVECR faults v11 WITHOUT clearing the FPSR exception byte
    (WinUAE faults before fpsr_clear_status) (157-159).

Fixed in ap040_core.v:
  - Nonexisting opmodes (WinUAE fault_if_nonexisting_opmode's 68040
    list) take an immediate F-line: format $0 vector 11 with FPIAR and
    FPSR untouched; opmodes $78-$7F take VECTOR 4 (160-163).  Existing
    but unimplemented opmodes keep the format-$2 FPSP route.
  - FPIAR updates only on paths that engage the FPU; malformed
    encodings that F-line out leave it alone.
  - FBcc/FScc/FDBcc/FTRAPcc predicate bit 5 ALIASES (fpp_cond masks
    0x1f; table68k defines FBcc for all 64 encodings) (164-165, 207).
  - FMOVEM register lists, 68040 rules: loads map mask bit 7 to FP0
    regardless of mode and reject -(An); stores reject (An)+ and
    PC-relative EAs; a store whose mask convention disagrees with its
    EA direction (control EA + pred mode, or -(An) + postinc mode)
    writes each register's three longwords REVERSED, low mantissa
    first (166-183).
  - Control-register moves: empty selection means FPIAR; An transfers
    legal for FPIAR only; immediate sources may load several registers;
    multi-register-to-Dn and other malformed encodings are F-line traps,
    not vector 4 (184-190).
  - The conditional-predicate logic was verified EQUAL to WinUAE's
    condition_table_040_060 for all 512 control/predicate combinations
    offline, and t_fpu 191-206 sweeps all 16 FPSR rows against the
    table words on every run.

The predicate sweep pushed the code section past $3200 where the data
area starts, silently corrupting test code; the battery therefore lives
in its own org $8000 section and failt uses jmp.  If t_fpu grows,
check the section sizes vasm prints.

Still deliberately unimplemented (dataset-invisible): nonmaskable
OVFL/UNFL traps with BUSY-frame FSAVE state, vector-55 datatype FSAVE
payloads, and INEX-on-away-rounding for deep extended underflow.
Revision-$41 vector-11 UNIMP frame contents, complete multiword transfer,
acknowledgement, FRESTORE round trip, and IDLE-state restoration are now
covered by t_fpu's directed checks 260-285.

WinUAE-oracle MMU and exception-frame audit 2026-08-07
------------------------------------------------------
Requested comparison of the MMU and exception frames against Musashi and
WinUAE.  Musashi's m68kmmu.h does not implement 040 PTEST at all, so the
oracle is WinUAE's cpummu.cpp/newcpu.cpp throughout.  The table walk
itself audited CLEAN: index arithmetic at every level, UDT/PDT decoding,
single-level indirect resolution, W-bit accumulation across levels, the
U/M history protocol including U-without-M on denied writes, S-protection
at access time, TTR matching (priority, S-field, E=0 behaviour) and
PFLUSH page/global variants with the G bit all match the oracle.

Fixed (t_mmu 48-59 plus updated 19/38/39, each negative-control proven):
  - MOVEC to TC no longer performs an implicit PFLUSHA.  Reads/writes of
    every MMU register leave both ATCs intact; t_mmu 36/37/60 proves the
    stale mapping survives TC and becomes visible only after explicit
    PFLUSHA (MC68040UM 3.7.4).
  - Warm RSTI clears only TC.E and the four TTR E bits.  TC.P, the other
    MMU register fields and all ATC entries survive; the directed reset
    test distinguishes this from deterministic FPGA cold initialization
    (MC68040UM 3.6.1).
  - PTEST on a TTR match returns exactly T|R ($3): the PA field stays
    clear.  A PTESTW against a write-protected TTR returns the B bit
    ($800) -- the old code returned $400, which is the G position.
  - PTEST runs the table search even with TC.E clear: the enable bit
    gates ordinary translation only.  t_exceptions' MMU-off PTEST decode
    test now sets up a real (invalid) root descriptor.
  - A physical bus error on a descriptor fetch during PTEST returns
    MMUSR = B.  tb_ap040_program gained a $F146 register that arms a
    one-shot walker bus error to test it.
  - PTEST's pre-flush drops the matching page from BOTH ATCs.
  - MOVE16 access errors report SSW SIZE=line with TT0, the EA aligned
    to the line, and the WB3 valid bit clear (WinUAE clears it in favour
    of a WB2 line writeback, which the pure-restart model omits).
  - TAS/CAS/CAS2 operand faults report SSW LK with RW clear.
  - Faulting MOVES to FC 0/3/4/7 reports TT=10 with the raw FC in TM;
    FC 2/6 is remapped onto the corresponding data space.
  - The format $7 frame now carries the fault address in EA, and for
    write faults a valid WB3 slot (WB3S = $80 | SSW low bits, WB3A = FA,
    WB3D = write data), matching WinUAE.  A handler that completes the
    writeback and RTEs will see the write repeated by the restart --
    the same behaviour WinUAE produces.

Documented deviations (unchanged, deliberately):
  - Invalid translations are NOT cached in the ATC.  Real 040 silicon
    caches the invalid entry and requires the handler to PFLUSH; this
    implementation re-walks instead, which is strictly more forgiving
    (a handler that fixes the table without PFLUSH works here, hangs on
    real silicon).  PTEST cannot observe the difference: it always
    performs a fresh search.
  - SSW CM/CT continuation is never generated (pure instruction
    restart); the RTE ignores those bits, so hand-crafted continuation
    frames are not honoured.
  - No WB2/WB1 writeback slots (always zero), consistent with restart.
  - The 68040's "always fill the ATC even on a failed search" detail is
    subsumed by the above.

WinUAE-oracle exception/stack-frame audit 2026-08-07 (second pass)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Requested comparison of the whole exception machinery against Musashi and
WinUAE.  The per-vector table audited CLEAN: every exc() call site matches
WinUAE's exception_pc() (stacked PC = the faulting instruction for vectors
2/3/4/8/10/11, the next instruction otherwise) and the format map (formats
